WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.878
Task statement 4.6.

2
00:00:01.878 --> 00:00:06.083
Design multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures.

3
00:00:06.083 --> 00:00:14.857
The session that wrote a 14-file change approves it; an independent reviewer finds the broken contract between two files.

4
00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:24.480
When the same session reviews its own code, it carries all its earlier assumptions, so it tends to see what it meant rather than what it wrote.

5
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.252
A separate Claude instance starts fresh and sees only the change and your criteria.

6
00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:34.369
It is the difference between proofreading your own essay and proofreading someone else's.

7
00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:37.892
Split a large review into passes.

8
00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:42.780
First, review each file by itself, so attention stays focused on local bugs.

9
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:49.726
Then run a separate integration pass that looks across files at contracts, data flow and shared assumptions.

10
00:00:49.726 --> 00:00:53.392
Asking for both in one giant pass spreads attention thin.

11
00:00:53.392 --> 00:00:57.508
Have each finding report a confidence value so you can route it.

12
00:00:58.908 --> 00:01:00.567
Confidence lets you route.

13
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:03.375
Post high confidence findings straight away.

14
00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:09.629
Send low confidence ones to a person or to a verifier that tries to confirm them against the code.

15
00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:21.372
Anthropic's own Code Review service follows a similar shape: several agents look for issues in parallel, then a verification step filters out false positives before anything is posted.

16
00:01:22.772 --> 00:01:27.664
In the continuous integration scenario, a pull request touches fourteen files.

17
00:01:27.664 --> 00:01:33.937
A separate reviewer instance, not the one that generated the code, runs a focused pass on each file.

18
00:01:33.937 --> 00:01:42.342
Then an integration pass looks across them and spots a field that was renamed in one file but still used under its old name elsewhere.

19
00:01:42.342 --> 00:01:44.788
Uncertain findings go to a human first.

20
00:01:46.188 --> 00:01:48.444
Look for independence and structure.

21
00:01:48.444 --> 00:01:52.955
A stronger review prompt in the same session is still the same reviewer.

22
00:01:52.955 --> 00:02:00.661
A single huge pass misses things a per file pass would catch, and it also misses what only an integration pass would catch.

23
00:02:00.661 --> 00:02:07.052
And treat every finding equally only if you want noise; confidence based routing is the better design.

24
00:02:08.452 --> 00:02:10.858
Separate the writer from the reviewer.

25
00:02:10.858 --> 00:02:14.911
Break big reviews into per file passes plus an integration pass.

26
00:02:14.911 --> 00:02:21.308
Attach confidence to every finding and use it to decide what gets posted and what gets checked first.

27
00:02:21.308 --> 00:02:24.537
That design finds more real issues with less noise.

